# QSPA modem selector -- without this, four QTI telephony apks never install.
#
# org.codeaurora.ims, com.qti.phone, com.qualcomm.qcrilmsgtunnel and
# com.qualcomm.qti.uimGbaApp are "QSPA" single-apk packages: each declares an
# <overlay> gated on a boot property,
#
#   requiredPropertyName='ro.boot.vendor.qspa.modem' requiredPropertyValue='enabled'
#
# and ApkLiteParseUtils drops any apk whose required system property does not
# match (frameworks/base/core/java/android/content/pm/parsing/
# ApkLiteParseUtils.java:803-810):
#
#   Failed to parse /system_ext/priv-app/qcrilmsgtunnel:
#     Skipping target and overlay pair com.qualcomm.qcrilmsgtunnel and <path>
#
# So the apks sat in /system_ext/priv-app and /product/app and never appeared in
# `pm list packages` at all -- for the entire life of this port. Setting this
# property and restarting the framework installed all four, and org.codeaurora.ims
# (the IMS app, i.e. VoLTE) and com.qti.phone ran for the first time.
#
# WHY AN RC FILE AND NOT A .prop OR THE CMDLINE:
#   * ro.boot.* is created ONLY from androidboot.* in the kernel cmdline or
#     bootconfig (property_service.cpp:1360-1375). A build.prop entry cannot make
#     one. Proof already in this tree: vendor.prop sets ro.boot.mmi.bootlog=true,
#     it reaches /vendor/build.prop:273, and the runtime property is EMPTY.
#   * BOARD_BOOTCONFIG and BOARD_KERNEL_CMDLINE both land in vendor_boot on this
#     header-v4 device, and install-lineage-fastboot.sh flashes only boot,
#     init_boot, super, vbmeta and vbmeta_system -- vendor_boot stays stock. Our
#     boot.img was checked with unpack_bootimg: "command line args:" is empty.
#     So both build-side vehicles are inert here.
#   * ro.boot.* is labelled u:object_r:bootloader_prop:s0 and vendor_init has no
#     permission to set it (zero matches in vendor_sepolicy.cil), so this file
#     MUST be installed to /system_ext/etc/init and run in the init context, not
#     /vendor/etc/init.
#
# If the bootloader ever supplies androidboot.vendor.qspa.modem itself, that value
# is set during PropertyInit() long before this runs and wins outright, because
# ro.* properties are write-once. This only fills in a value the bootloader on
# this unit does not provide.

on early-init
    setprop ro.boot.vendor.qspa.modem enabled
